TI  - Construction defects, envelope degradation and lifecycle carbon implications for building energy performance: evidence from Lagos, Nigeria
T2  - ENERGY AND BUILDINGS
LA  - English
KW  - Building energy performance
KW  - Thermal envelope degradation
KW  - Lifecycle carbon-energy interaction
KW  - Construction defects
KW  - BIM-based performance assessment
AB  - Little attention has been given to the quality of construction as a potential influence on the energy and carbon performance of buildings in many developing economies. In addition to causing post-construction defects that compromise building envelopes, poor construction quality reduces the long-term operational efficiency of building materials. In this paper, we reconceptualize construction defects as indirect drivers of energy and carbon use by integrating defect severity, repair frequency, and lifecycle considerations into a Quality-Sustainability Integration Index (QSII). We employed a mixed-methods approach comprising a survey of 83 construction professionals in Nigeria and a longitudinal review of maintenance records for 14 residential, commercial, and institutional buildings in Lagos, Nigeria. BIM-based Lifecycle Assessment was used to quantify the embodied carbon associated with defect-induced repair cycles, and a conceptual Digital Twin prototype was developed to visualize defect propagation pathways and intervention strategies. Results indicate that envelope- and waterproofing-related defects generate disproportionately high lifecycle carbon emissions and indirect operational energy penalties due to thermal degradation in hot-humid climates. Findings are calibrated for Lagos's hothumid climate and institutional context; application to other climatic or regulatory settings requires local empirical validation and parameter recalibration. By positioning construction quality as a latent lever for energy efficiency rather than solely a management issue, this research advances building performance discourse beyond the design stage and emphasizes defect prevention for long-term sustainability.
