TI  - Automated procedural modeling for tunnel inspection and digital twin development
T2  - TUNNELLING AND UNDERGROUND SPACE TECHNOLOGY
LA  - English
KW  - Digital twin
KW  - Infrastructure management
KW  - Modeling
KW  - Tunnel lifecycle
KW  - Inspection
KW  - MAINTENANCE STRATEGY
KW  - STATE
AB  - The creation of structured informational models improves understanding of tunnel behavior throughout its service life and enables faster detection of defects, which is critical for hazard assessment and infrastructure safety. Recent studies highlight the importance of automation and 3D reconstruction in overcoming the limitations of manual tunnel inspection and data management. Technologies such as Point Cloud Data (PCD) and advanced modeling techniques have enhanced inspection processes and model generation. However, significant challenges persist in the development of Digital Twins (DT), particularly in the integration of heterogeneous data sources. This paper presents a methodology that combines parametric modeling technics with field data to develop an automated procedural modeling framework for tunnel inspection Digital Twins that integrates heterogeneous data and optimizes lifecycle management. To support this goal, the framework also aims to: (i) automate modeling workflows; (ii) enable the creation of a structured databank; (iii) improve inspection accuracy; and (iv) introduce a new index, the Mesh Decimation Quality (MDQ), to evaluate mesh quality during the decimation and digitization process. The approach was validated through a case study of the Coastal Tunnel in Brazil, for which an automated digital twin was created using PCD from the Faro Focus 3D X330 laser scanner. The MDQ index was applied to evaluate mesh resolution, ensuring model accuracy and controlling computational costs. Metadata from the physical asset was integrated using procedural code scripts. A proposed digital inspection approach, utilizing a procedural inspection grid, successfully identified defects and classified areas, demonstrating the method efficiency and accuracy. This framework highlights the feasibility of digitalizing tunnel inspections and integrating real-world data for improved infrastructure management, with potential for future expansion.
