TI  - A transformer-based surrogate modeling strategy for tunnel digital twin in full-field displacement prediction under adjacent tunnel construction
T2  - ADVANCED ENGINEERING INFORMATICS
LA  - English
KW  - Digital twin
KW  - Shield tunnel
KW  - Surrogate modeling
KW  - Transformer
KW  - Cross-attention fusion
KW  - Adjacent excavation
AB  - Accurately predicting ground settlement and tunnel lining deformation is essential for ensuring the safety and efficiency of shield tunnel projects, especially in urban areas affected by adjacent excavations. Rapid and reliable forecasts of tunnel behavior support proactive design decisions and risk mitigation. However, most existing surrogate models offer limited predictive resolution and accuracy, as they predict responses only at sparse monitoring points and ignore spatial information, which restricts their applicability in building comprehensive digital twin (DT) systems for full-field simulation. To address these limitations, this study proposes a DT framework for predicting ground settlement and lining deformation in shield tunnels affected by adjacent excavation. A hybrid surrogate model, called the Dual-Fusion Swin-Table Transformer Network (DF-STTN), is developed by combining Swin Transformer and TabTransformer to handle spatial and non-spatial inputs. To enhance multimodal interaction, a dual-stage fusion strategy is applied. The model is trained on high-fidelity finite element data, then updated and validated using results from laboratory-scale tunnel excavation tests. Results demonstrate that the inclusion of spatial information improves prediction accuracy, with notable improvements achieved when three measurement points of the tunnel are used. DF-STTN outperforms conventional convolution-based and single-fusion models in both ground settlement and lining deformation tasks. The proposed framework achieves high predictive accuracy and generalization capability for early-stage tunnel design and excavation risk assessment.
